"Step into an elevator, press a button, and a small room carries you and ten other people up the building. At Taipei 101, the elevator takes visitors from the 5th floor to the 89th in only 37 seconds. A full elevator car can weigh more than a family car. How can a motor lift something that heavy, again and again, all day long? The secret is hidden in the shaft, where riders never see it. Strong steel cables are tied to the top of the car. They go up, over a wheel at the top of the building, and down the other side. There they hold a tall stack of heavy metal blocks called the counterweight. When the car goes up, the counterweight comes down. When the car comes down, the counterweight goes up. Think of a seesaw with two children of the same weight. A tiny push sends one up and the other down. An elevator works the same way. The counterweight weighs about as much as the car plus half of the riders it can carry. With the car half full, the two sides balance almost perfectly, and the motor hardly has to work. So the motor never lifts the whole car. It only moves the difference between the two sides. In our model, a full car weighs 1,840 kilograms, but the motor has to hold only 390 kilograms. An empty car is lighter than the counterweight, so it wants to rise by itself, and the motor holds it back. Either way, the elevator uses far less energy. What if a cable breaks? Long ago, people were afraid of exactly that. In 1853 an American, Elisha Otis, invented a safety brake. The next year, at a fair in New York, he stood on a platform high in the air and had the rope cut. The platform stopped at once. His brake gripped the rails on both sides and held him there. Elevators today are even safer. A car hangs on several cables, and each cable alone is strong enough to hold a full car. A device called a governor watches the speed. If the car ever moves down too fast, brakes on the car grip the rails and stop it. So ride without fear, but in a fire or after an earthquake, always 